%S A398869 0,0,0,0,0,0,1,0,1,0,1,1,2,1,1,2,2
%N A398869 Minimum number of L-seams needed to sew an optimal Mrs. Perkins's quilt of side length n.
%C A398869 Though Mrs. Perkins's quilt is a lovely math problem, it might prove difficult to sew. A quilter may want to minimize the number of L-seams, also known as inset seams, where the piece must be turned at a corner; these seams can pucker. a(n) is the minimum number of L-seams needed to sew an optimal Mrs. Perkins's quilt of side n, over all optimal dissections and all sewing orders.
%C A398869 To minimize L-seams, a quilt is sewn by joining two units at a time along their entire shared boundary (seams are often pressed before the next is sewn). If a join must turn a corner so that the corner is inset into the joined unit, it is an L-seam.
%C A398869 Another way to think about this is if you want to cut the quilt apart, what is the minimum number of corner cuts required to maximize guillotine cuts. This has applications in the cutting of paper and other materials.
%C A398869 Each seam is either straight or turns exactly one corner (an L-seam), and the shared boundary being sewn must form a single connected path (one cannot sew around a piece it encloses).
%C A398869 a(n) = 0 if and only if some optimal dissection of the n X n square is a guillotine (slicing) dissection.
%C A398869 The data from squaring.net was used to get the number of squares and then all quilts were generated for each n to visually check sizes 1 through 17. Many quilts of the same size n are similar to each other and can be grouped to manually check for L-seams. Python code in the links can be used to generate quilts for n<=17.
%D A398869 H. E. Dudeney, Amusements in Mathematics, Nelson, London, 1917, pages 47 and 180 (Problem 173, "Mrs. Perkins's Quilt"); reprinted by Dover, New York.
%H A398869 Stuart E. Anderson, <a href="https://www.squaring.net/quilts/mrs-perkins-quilts.html">Mrs Perkins's Quilt</a>, Squaring.net (quilt data computed and compiled by Ed Pegg Jr, Stuart Anderson, James Williams, Lorenz Milla, Ed Wynn, Richard Guy, and others).
%H A398869 Stuart E. Anderson, <a href="https://www.squaring.net/downloads/MrsPerkinssQuilts.txt.zip">Mrs Perkins's Quilts data file</a> (zip), Squaring.net.
%H A398869 Ed Pegg Jr, <a href="https://www.mathpuzzle.com/MAA/06-Square%20Packing/mathgames_12_01_03.html">Math Games: Square Packing</a>, MAA Online, December 1, 2003.
%H A398869 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/MrsPerkinssQuilt.html">Mrs. Perkins's Quilt</a>.
%H A398869 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Squaring_the_square">Squaring the square</a>.
%H A398869 Sophia Wood, <a href="/A398869/a398869.py.txt">Python to generate n size quilts</a>.
%H A398869 Sophia Wood, <a href="https://www.fractalkitty.com/rabbit-hole-minimum-l-seams/">Rabbit Hole: Minimum L-seams</a>.
%H A398869 Ed Wynn, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.5420">Exhaustive generation of 'Mrs Perkins's quilt' square dissections for low orders</a>, arXiv:1308.5420 [math.CO], 2013-2014.
%e A398869 For n = 7, a(7) = 1: one L-seam (#) is required to sew this quilt, around the 3 X 3 block in the lower corner. All four optimal quilts of side 7 (up to symmetry) give the same result, so no choice of quilt avoids the L-seam.
